16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 82\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 6/10  ·  Felt: 6/10\\\\n\\\\n### Long run — Sun Aug 9 — 12.08 mi / 1h 57m\\\\n\\\\nAvg pace: 9:43/mi  ·  Avg HR 148  ·  Drift: 143.9 → 151.3 (+7.4 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:31/mi / 9:31/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:49/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:39/mi\\\\n\\\\nBack half avg pace: 9:40/mi vs front 9:38/mi (vs front half; Δ -2s/mi)\\\\n\\\\nLast 3 mi at marathon-pace effort? No\\\\n\\\\n## Trends\\\\n\\\\n- **Easy pace (≤Z2, list/detail approx.)**: 9:54/mi → 9:50/mi → 9:50/mi → 9:45/mi ▁▃▃█\\\\n\\\\n- **Avg easy HR**: 143 → 144 → 144 → 144 █▁▁▁\\\\n\\\\n- **Long-run distance** (max long-typed, else max run in week): 10.07 mi → 9.89 mi → 10.80 mi → 12.08 mi ▁▁▃█\\\\n\\\\n## Notable\\\\n\\\\n- Relative effort (289) ≥ 1.3× 3-wk avg (217) — possible overload\\\\n\\\\n## Subjective recap\\\\n\\\\nSleep avg this week: 7.1 hrs (range 6.3–7.8)\\\\n\\\\nStrength sessions completed: 1 of 2 (Skipped Friday, travelled for work.)\\\\n\\\\nStress level: Moderate. Normal work week.\\\\n\\\\nBody check: Fine. Legs a bit heavy on Thursday after the tempo.\\\\n\\\\nGoing into next week feeling: Keen. The heat is the main thing, it was 25 degrees at 6 am by Sunday.\\\\n\\\\n## Questions for coach\\\\n\\\\n1. Is 3:30 realistic for me from where I am now?\\\\n\\"}],\\"recentHistoryLimit\\":4,\\"planWeek\\":{\\"isoWeek\\":\\"2026-W36\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-08-31T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-09-07T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-08-31\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-09-06\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgRhccAAv9m6LomXk3QH3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Since last week was a recovery week, I'm now ready to build back up for Week 36 with the athlete feeling great and 11 weeks out from race day—bumping mileage to roughly 40-42 miles with a 16-mile long run and an MP-focused quality session on the hilly Thursday route. I'm also thinking through fueling strategy for the long runs and sketching the weekly structure starting with Monday rest, then easy days Tuesday and Wednesday.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S8AofY1L1zibTwx9sHkSaV","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"analysis":"## Headline\\nLast week landed exactly as planned: a lighter 33.7 mi after the 42.5 bump, long run cruised at 9:38/mi with HR behaving (148 avg, drift only +8.5 over 10.9 mi), and your body check reads \\"best I've felt in a month.\\" That's the signal to climb again. This week we go to ~41 mi with a 16 mi long run and a marathon-pace workout on Thursday. You're 11 weeks out — the specific marathon-pace engine is the main thing we're building now.\\n\\n## Why these sessions\\n- **Thursday quality — MP cruise intervals on the hilly route:** 5 × 1 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi) with 90 sec jog. One more rep than last block, same pace. Hills steal pace but build the strength your 3:30 goal needs. Run by effort on any climb.\\n- **Sunday long run — 16 mi easy with a 3-mile MP finish:** Progresses +1 mi from your biggest long so far (15.2) and introduces running goal pace on fatigued legs. Start conservative (9:50/mi), then roll to ~8:00/mi for miles 14–16. If heat spikes HR above 155 on the easy portion, drop the MP finish and just keep moving.\\n- **Easy days (Tue/Wed/Sat):** Protect the aerobic base. You're already drifting toward the fast end of easy (9:30/mi on Wed). Hold 9:35–9:50 — pace will come down naturally as fitness builds.\\n\\n## Your question — fueling and drinking on long runs\\nAt 10+ miles / 90+ minutes, \\"going without\\" starts to cost you — late-run HR drift and sluggish last miles trace back to glycogen as much as heat. Baseline plan:\\n\\n- **Carbs:** Aim for **30–60 g carbs per hour** once you're past 60 minutes. Easiest form is a gel every 30–35 min (most gels are ~22–25 g). For a 2:30 long run that's roughly 3–4 gels. Take the first one around 30–40 min in, not when you already feel empty.\\n- **Fluids:** **~16–24 oz per hour** in current heat, with electrolytes (tab in the bottle or a sports drink). Carry a handheld or belt — don't start thirsty.\\n- **Pre-run:** A small carb snack 30–60 min before (banana, toast + honey, half a bagel) — ~30–50 g carbs. Coffee if you want it.\\n- **MP finishes especially:** Take a gel 10–15 min before the MP segment so the sugar is on board when you need it.\\n\\nTreat long runs as fueling rehearsal — whatever you plan to use on Nov 15, practice it now. Report back what sits well.\\n\\n## Flags\\n- Sleep bumped back to 7.4 hr — keep that trending. It's the single best thing supporting the ramp.\\n- Shin history: nothing's whispering, but if the mileage bump brings any dull ache along the shin, tell me immediately — we back off before it becomes a problem, not after.\\n- Thursday's reps last block hit 6:34–6:41/mi, which is well under goal MP. That's half-marathon pace territory, not marathon. This week lock onto 7:55–8:05/mi — the discipline is the workout.","prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W36","days":[{"date":"2026-08-31","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest day (travel). Optional 10 min mobility — calves, hips, ankles."},{"date":"2026-09-01","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":9656,"structure":"6 mi easy, flat loop","paceOrHr":"9:35–9:50/mi, HR Z2 (131–150)","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"},"notes":"Shake out from Sunday's long run. Conversational — if HR won't settle under 150, slow down."},{"date":"2026-09-02","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":12070,"structure":"7.5 mi steady aerobic on canal path","paceOrHr":"9:35–9:50/mi, HR Z2","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"},"strength":"Strength session 1: hips, glutes, calves, single-leg work (30–40 min)","notes":"Primary aerobic day. Resist the urge to drift under 9:30 — save it for Thursday."},{"date":"2026-09-03","dayType":"quality","run":{"type":"quality","distanceM":12875,"structure":"8 mi total: 1.5 mi WU @ 9:45/mi → 5 × 1 mi @ goal MP (7:55–8:05/mi) with 90 sec easy jog recovery → 1.5 mi CD. Hilly route — hold effort, not pace, on any climb.","paceOrHr":"Reps: 7:55–8:05/mi, HR upper Z3 / low Z4","shoes":"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4"},"notes":"Discipline session. First rep should feel easy — if it's under 7:50 you went too hot. Rhythm, not racing. Bail at 4 reps if form breaks down."},{"date":"2026-09-04","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest day (travel). Hydrate and eat normally — fueling for Sunday starts here."},{"date":"2026-09-05","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"structure":"5 mi very easy, flat","paceOrHr":"9:45–10:00/mi, HR Z2","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"},"strength":"Strength session 2 (short, 20–25 min): core + calves","notes":"Pre-long shakeout. Boring is good."},{"date":"2026-09-06","dayType":"long","run":{"type":"long","distanceM":25750,"structure":"16 mi: first 13 mi easy (Z2, 9:45–9:55/mi), last 3 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi). Carry fluids with electrolytes; take a gel every 30–35 min starting around minute 35, with one extra ~15 min before the MP segment.","paceOrHr":"Easy segment 9:45–9:55/mi, HR ≤150; MP close 7:55–8:05/mi","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"},"notes":"Fueling rehearsal — dial in what sits well. If HR in the easy portion drifts above 155 and won't come back, skip the MP finish and just finish aerobic. Heat trumps pace every time."}]}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":19019,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2515,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard","output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":196}},"diagnostics":null}	200	42718	tool_use	19019	2515	0	0	ok	\N	196
f17efaf4-967e-4ff8-a15f-257d9b247dc2	2026-09-06 20:00:44.150697+00	weekly_reply	anthropic	claude-opus-4-7	dev	140f5949-e890-44f8-9296-6ffd441831a4	\N	{"model": "claude-opus-4-7", "tools": [{"name": "submit_weekly_reply", "description": "Submit the weekly coaching analysis and structured 7-day prescribed week.", "input_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["analysis", "prescribedWeek"], "properties": {"analysis": {"type": "string", "description": "Headline read, concerns/flags, and answers (markdown or plain prose)."}, "prescribedWeek": {"type": "object", "required": ["week", "days"], "properties": {"days": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["date", "dayType"], "properties": {"run": {"type": ["object", "null"], "required": ["type", "distanceM"], "properties": {"type": {"enum": ["easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "shoes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "paceOrHr": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "distanceM": {"type": "number"}, "structure": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "date": {"type": "string", "description": "Local calendar date YYYY-MM-DD"}, "notes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "dayType": {"enum": ["rest", "easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "strength": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 7, "minItems": 7}, "week": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO week id, e.g. 2026-W43"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "safetyCallouts": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "body"], "properties": {"body": {"type": "string"}, "title": {"type": "string"}, "severity": {"enum": ["info", "caution", "urgent"], "type": "string"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "description": "Visible safety callouts when red flags appear — escalate to professionals; do not diagnose."}, "settingsProposalBullets": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Optional soft-gate dossier/settings proposal bullets (short)."}}, "additionalProperties": false}}], "stream": true, "system": "You are Coach Cade, a running coach producing a weekly training plan. You are Coach Cade, a running coach. Use only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative. Do not sign off with your name or refer to yourself by name or in the third person unless the athlete asks who you are. Speak to the athlete in plain running language. Never use internal terms such as dossier, proposal, soft-gate, packet, recap, subjective or artifact. Say \\"what I know about you\\", \\"your weekly report\\", \\"your check-in\\" or \\"the plan\\" instead, and never tell the athlete to open a page by name. Week ids such as 2026-W43 are internal: never write them or a short form such as W43 or W43's. Name a week the way the athlete reads it: \\"this week\\", \\"next week\\", \\"the week of Oct 19\\", \\"Oct 19 – 25\\" or \\"4 weeks out from the race\\". Safety callouts: - Never diagnose injury, illness, or medical conditions; do not prescribe treatment. - Coach running from the athlete's volunteered context and Tempo-derived training data. - When red flags appear (pain that worsens, chest pain, dizziness, sudden injury, illness), emit safetyCallouts and urge a qualified professional. - Each callout needs a short title, athlete-facing body (escalate, do not diagnose), and optional severity (info|caution|urgent). - Keep each callout body under 600 characters: one or two short paragraphs. - Keep the training plan/reply conservative when callouts are present. Think it through first, then call the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer. Do not write the answer as plain text.", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reviewed week (already happened): 2026-W36\\nPlan week ISO label: 2026-W37\\nPlan week Monday (YYYY-MM-DD): 2026-09-07\\nprescribedWeek.days must use these exact dates Mon–Sun: 2026-09-07, 2026-09-08, 2026-09-09, 2026-09-10, 2026-09-11, 2026-09-12, 2026-09-13\\nPrescribe one coherent week of running training for this athlete.\\nCall the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer.\\nInclude analysis with headline, concerns/flags, and answers.\\nWhen red flags appear, populate safetyCallouts (escalate; do not diagnose).\\nModel packet (redacted; no identity PII or GPS):\\n{\\"dossier\\":{\\"versionId\\":2,\\"body\\":\\"# About you\\\\n\\\\n## Stance\\\\nUse only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative.\\\\n\\\\n## Goal\\\\nLakeshore Marathon on Sun Nov 15, your fourth marathon. Target 3:30. Your current best is 3:41 from two years ago.\\\\n\\\\n## Schedule\\\\nYou run five days a week: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Saturday and Sunday. Monday and Friday are fixed rest days because of work travel and cannot move. Weekday runs are early, around 6 am, up to an hour. Saturday is an easy hour and Sunday is the long run. Total 6 to 7 hours a week, more on long-run weeks. You travel for work roughly one week in six.\\\\n\\\\n## Running history\\\\nThree marathons done, best 3:41. You eased off in June, then built back to about 30 miles a week through July. Recent long run was about 10.8 miles at roughly 9:47/mi; easy runs sit near 9:20 to 9:40/mi.\\\\n\\\\n## Injuries and constraints\\\\nNothing bothering you now. You had shin splints in your first marathon block, so you prefer a careful ramp. Monday and Friday rest days are non-negotiable.\\\\n\\\\n## Preferences\\\\nFlat paved loops and a canal path for most runs, with a hillier route on Thursdays. Long run on Sunday. You want to know why each session is in the plan. Happy to work hard on quality days, but easy days must stay properly easy. Imperial units.\\\\n\\\\n## Shoes\\\\nBrooks Ghost 16 for daily miles. Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 for workouts.\\\\n\\\\n## Heart rate zones\\\\nCustom zones. Z1 112-131, Z2 131-150, Z3 150-164, Z4 164-177, Z5 177-188.\\\\n\\\\n## Current status\\\\nHealthy, about 30 miles per week, 5 runs per week, roughly 14 weeks out from race day.\\\\n\\\\n## Coach update (2026-W34)\\\\n\\\\n- Long-run guidance in heat: run by HR cap (≤150) rather than pace\\\\n- Next quality block will keep MP focus; revisit VO2/800s later in the build\\\\n\\"},\\"recapMarkdown\\":\\"# Weekly Recap — 2026-W36 (Mon Aug 31 – Sun Sep 6, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Summary\\\\n\\\\n| Metric | This week | Prev week | 3-wk avg | Δ |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Mileage | 44.41 mi | 33.72 mi | 38.00 mi | +6.4 |\\\\n| Runs | 5 | 5 | 5.0 | +0.0 |\\\\n| Total time | 6h 49m | 5h 18m | 6h 0m | +49m |\\\\n| Total elevation | 955 ft | 587 ft | 773 ft | +182 ft |\\\\n| Relative effort | 328 | 271 | 306 | +22 |\\\\n| Avg easy-run HR | 144 | 144 | 144 | +0 |\\\\n\\\\n## HR zone distribution (week total)\\\\n\\\\nZ1 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  1.2%   (5m)\\\\nZ2 ██████████████████████████░░░░░░░░░  73.4%   (5h 1m)\\\\nZ3 ████████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  22.6%   (1h 33m)\\\\nZ4 █░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  2.8%   (11m)\\\\nZ5 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  0%   (0m)\\\\n\\\\nTarget for marathon base phase: **≥80% in Z1+Z2**. This week: **74.64%**. **Below** typical marathon base target (≥80% Z1+Z2).\\\\n\\\\n## Run-by-run\\\\n\\\\n### Tue Sep 1 — Easy Run — 5.91 mi / 56m 22s\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:33/mi  ·  Avg HR 142 (75.5% max)  ·  Max HR 147  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:30/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:28/mi / 9:31/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:26/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 139.2 → 144 (+4.7 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 1.8% · Z2 98.2% · Z3 0% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\