/mi; MP close 7:55–8:05/mi", "distanceM": 19312, "structure": "12 mi: first 10 mi easy (Z2), last 2 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi). Start out ~9:50/mi, let HR settle, then roll into MP for the final 2. Carry fluids and start before sunrise if possible."}, "date": "2026-08-16", "notes": "Practice marathon fueling: a gel every 30–35 min. Bail on the MP finish if HR is drifting into Z4 — heat trumps pace targets.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W33"}	## Headline\nStrong week of Aug 3–9: 35.8 mi, long run out to 12.1 mi, and 83% of time in Z1+Z2 — exactly the aerobic base we want 14 weeks out. Thursday's tempo was quality work and the Sunday long run was steady with modest drift. Nice job.\n\nBecause mileage jumped ~10% and relative effort (289) was well above your 3-week average (217), the week of Aug 10–16 is a **consolidation week at the same volume** — no mileage bump. We'll sharpen one quality session, keep easy days properly easy (especially in the heat), and then push the long run again the following week.\n\n## Flags / things I'm watching\n- **Heat**: 25°C at 6 am on Sunday is real. Z3 creep on Wed and Sun long run (both ~17–20% Z3) is likely heat as much as effort. Start the easy days 10–15 sec/mi slower when it's muggy and let HR, not pace, lead.\n- **Thursday tempo pace**: 7:45–7:52/mi is faster than goal marathon pace (3:30 ≈ 8:00/mi) and closer to half-marathon effort. Fine occasionally, but this week I want you to practice *marathon* pace so your body learns that gear.\n- **Shin splint history**: nothing flaring now, but we hold volume this week rather than stacking another jump on top of last week's +4.3 mi.\n- **Strength**: 1 of 2 done. Try to get both in — hips/calves/feet are your shin insurance.\n\n## Your question — is 3:30 realistic?\nHonest answer: it's a stretch but not unreasonable. 3:30 is ~8:00/mi. Your easy runs at 9:20–9:40 and HR ~144 (76% max) suggest aerobic fitness is building nicely, and your tempo splits at 7:45–7:52 show you have the raw speed. The gap from a 3:41 PR to 3:30 is 11 minutes, which is big but doable in 14 weeks given you're healthy and ramping sensibly. I'd call it a stretch "A" goal, with 3:35 as a very realistic "B". We'll know more after a couple of MP-specific long runs in September — I'll re-benchmark then.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out after Sunday's 12, pure Z2.\n- **Wed easy 6 + strides**: aerobic volume plus 6×20s strides to keep leg turnover sharp without taxing you.\n- **Thu quality — MP intervals on your hilly route**: 4 × 1 mi at goal marathon pace (8:00/mi) with 90s jog. Shorter and more pace-specific than last week's tempo. Hills on recoveries are fine.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long-run flush, keep it slow.\n- **Sun long 12 with MP finish**: same distance as last week, but finish the last 2 miles at ~8:00/mi. Teaches you to close strong on tired legs.\n\nTotal ~35 mi, same as last week. We'll bump to ~37–38 the week after.	\N	2026-08-09 20:00:56.171902+00	2026-08-09 20:00:56.171902+00	[{"body": "25°C at 6 am is enough to drive HR up and mask true effort. Start runs earlier where you can, carry fluids on anything over 45 min, and let HR (not pace) lead your easy days — if Z2 means 10:00/mi this week, that's the right call. If you ever feel dizzy, stop sweating, get a pounding headache, or feel nauseated on a run, stop and cool down; seek medical help if symptoms don't resolve quickly.", "title": "Heat and early-morning humidity", "severity": "caution"}]
673db0ec-b22b-4732-9a14-3b48998783a8	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-17", "notes": "Travel/rest day. Walk if you can, otherwise fully off.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40–9:55/mi, HR in Z2 (131–150)", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi easy, flat loop"}, "date": "2026-08-18", "notes": "Shake-out. Properly easy — nose-breathing pace.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35–9:50/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi easy, canal path"}, "date": "2026-08-19", "notes": "Steady aerobic. If HR creeps into Z3, back off pace.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP reps ~8:00/mi, HR upper Z3/low Z4", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, then 4 × 1 mi @ 8:00/mi (goal MP) with 2:00 jog recovery, 1.5 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-08-20", "notes": "Hillier Thursday route is fine — run reps by effort on any rises. First mile should feel controlled, not racing. If pace drifts under 7:50, pull back; this is a rhythm session, not a time trial.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-08-21", "notes": "Travel/rest. Full off day.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:00/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi easy"}, "date": "2026-08-22", "notes": "Pre-long shakeout. Keep it boring.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "Target 9:40–9:55/mi in cool conditions; in heat, add 15–30 sec/mi to hold HR ≤150", "distanceM": 22531, "structure": "14 mi easy, steady effort"}, "date": "2026-08-23", "notes": "Fuel every 30–40 min, carry fluid, start before sunrise if you can. Back half should not be slower by more than ~15 sec/mi — if it is, we dial future long runs by HR.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W34"}	## Headline\nYou're building nicely — 14 weeks out, 37.7 mi last week, long run up to 13 mi at 9:41/mi with HR behaving (144 easy, no upward trend). This week we hold mileage roughly flat (~38 mi), swap the 800s for marathon-pace work (addresses your concern that reps went too hot early), and push the long run to 14 mi by effort, not pace.\n\n## Why these sessions\n- **Thursday quality — MP intervals on the hilly route**: You flagged the 800s felt too hot early. At 14 weeks out we don't need pure VO2; we need the marathon-pace engine. 4 × 1 mi @ ~8:00/mi (your 3:30 target pace) teaches pacing discipline and rhythm with low injury cost. Hills give strength without the pounding of flat speed.\n- **Sunday long run — 14 mi easy**: Progressing +1 mi from last week. Keep it a conversational Z2 effort. In heat, HR tells the truth and pace lies.\n- **Easy days stay easy**: Your Z1+Z2 was 81% last week — right on target. Keep Tue/Wed/Sat honest at 9:40–9:50/mi so Thursday and Sunday can land.\n\n## Answer to your question\n**Should I slow the long run in the heat?** Yes — run by effort/HR, not pace. In 25°C+ at 6 am, expect to add 15–30 sec/mi versus a cool day to keep HR in Z2 (131–150). If HR drifts above 155 and won't come back, walk 30 sec at aid stops or cut it short. Pace will come back when the weather does; aerobic time on feet is what we're banking.\n\n## Flags\n- Last week's relative effort (304) is climbing fast — three straight weeks of +4 mi. We hold mileage flat this week to let adaptations catch up before the next bump.\n- Sleep averaging 6.9 hr is on the low side. If you can nudge toward 7.5 on quality-day eves, Thursday will feel better.\n- Shin history noted — nothing bothering you now, but if anything starts whispering, tell me early.	{"Long-run guidance in heat: run by HR cap (≤150) rather than pace","Next quality block will keep MP focus; revisit VO2/800s later in the build"}	2026-08-16 20:00:36.221627+00	2026-08-16 20:00:36.221627+00	\N
cc5d18d4-c308-481b-b011-dd3c99e03ba0	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-24", "notes": "Fixed rest. Prioritize sleep tonight — you're down on it. 10 min easy mobility if you feel like moving.", "dayType": "rest", "strength": "Optional mobility: ankles, hips, calves (10 min)"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40–10:00/mi, Z2 (HR 138–148)", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi conversational on the canal path. Let HR lead if it's humid."}, "date": "2026-08-25", "notes": "Shake-out. Keep it genuinely easy — no pushing the pace even if you feel fresh.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30–9:50/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi easy + 6×20s strides on flat with full walk recovery after the run. Strides are relaxed, controlled turnover — not sprints."}, "date": "2026-08-26", "notes": "Try for strength tonight — the week is lighter on purpose to let you fit it in.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 1 (30–40 min): hips, glutes, calves, single-leg work"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP reps 7:55–8:05/mi; WU/CD 9:40–10:00/mi", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi total on the hilly route. 2 mi warm-up → 3 × 1 mi @ goal MP (8:00/mi) with 90s easy jog between → 1.5 mi cool-down. Hold effort on uphill reps, don't force pace."}, "date": "2026-08-27", "notes": "Short and sharp. If the 2nd rep is already a struggle, pull the plug after 2 — this is a cutback week and nothing is proved by grinding.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-08-28", "notes": "Fixed rest. Hydrate and eat normally — fueling for Sunday starts today.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi very easy on flat canal path."}, "date": "2026-08-29", "notes": "Pre-long flush. Slower is better here.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 2 (20–25 min): core + calves. Skip if legs are flat."}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "HR ≤150 (mid-Z2); pace will likely land 9:40–9:55/mi", "distanceM": 19312, "structure": "12 mi steady, HR-capped. If HR drifts above 150, walk 30s and resume slower. No fast finish this week — this is recovery-oriented aerobic volume."}, "date": "2026-08-30", "notes": "Cutback long run. HR cap, no MP work. Fuel every 30–35 min with a gel to keep practicing race fueling. Start before sunrise if it's going to be warm.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W35"}	## Headline\nBig week — 42.5 mi, long run out to 15.2, and still 82% in Z1+Z2. Impressive work, especially stacked on top of a tough stretch at work (late nights, 6.4 hrs sleep, Saturday felt like a slog). But that's also a +7 mi jump over your 3-week average and relative effort climbed from 272 to 342. You asked for an easier week — you've earned it, and the training calls for it. This week of Aug 24–30 is a planned cutback: ~36 mi, long run back to 12, and one marathon-pace session that's crisp but short. We'll push again next week from a fresher starting point.\n\n## Flags / things I'm watching\n- **Load spike + low sleep + high stress**: classic combo that precedes a niggle, especially with your shin-splint history. Nothing hurts yet, so we take the cutback *now* rather than after a problem shows up.\n- **Long-run HR drift**: 144 → 151 over 15 miles on Sunday is on the higher side — the heat is biting. Keep the HR cap of 150 on long runs for now; let pace fall where it does.\n- **Thursday tempo**: 7:39–7:47/mi is faster than goal MP (8:00/mi). You have the top-end gear; this week I want you back on honest MP so the pace feels automatic.\n- **Strength**: 1 of 2 again. On a down week with less fatigue, two short sessions are very doable.\n\n## Your question — handling missed runs when you travel\nSimple rules, in priority order:\n1. **Never skip the long run** if you can help it. If travel kills Sunday, move it to Saturday or do it on the road the day you fly.\n2. **Protect one quality session per week**. If the Thursday workout is at risk, shift it to Wednesday or Tuesday and make the other day easy.\n3. **Easy miles are the most droppable.** If you lose 1–2 easy days to travel, don't try to cram them in — just land on the plane rested and resume.\n4. **Don't stack a missed long run onto the next week.** Pick up where the plan is, don't try to "make up" miles — that's how injuries happen.\n5. Give me a heads-up on travel weeks in your check-in and I'll shape the plan around the fixed slots you'll have.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out, pure Z2.\n- **Wed easy 7 + strides**: aerobic volume plus 6×20s strides for leg turnover without stress.\n- **Thu quality — 3 × 1 mi at MP**: shorter rep set on the hilly route. Goal is to groove 8:00/mi, not to grind.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 12, HR-capped**: distance comes down intentionally. Run by HR (≤150), let pace be whatever it is. No MP finish this week — the point is recovery adaptation.\n\nTotal ~36 mi (down from 42.5). Next week we rebuild toward ~40 with an MP long run.	\N	2026-08-23 20:00:51.339739+00	2026-08-23 20:00:51.339739+00	[{"body": "You've had a few weeks of climbing mileage, lower sleep (6.4 hrs avg), and high work stress. Nothing is hurting now, which is the right time to pull back — not after. If you notice shin tenderness, aching along the bone, or any pain that worsens during or after a run, pause running and check in with a sports physio or doctor rather than training through it. Don't try to make up this week's miles later.", "title": "Load + sleep combo — watch the shins", "severity": "caution"}]
140f5949-e890-44f8-9296-6ffd441831a4	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-31", "notes": "Fixed rest day (travel). Optional 10 min mobility — calves, hips, ankles.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35–9:50/mi, HR Z2 (131–150)", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi easy, flat loop"}, "date": "2026-09-01", "notes": "Shake out from Sunday's long run. Conversational — if HR won't settle under 150, slow down.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35–9:50/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 12070, "structure": "7.5 mi steady aerobic on canal path"}, "date": "2026-09-02", "notes": "Primary aerobic day. Resist the urge to drift under 9:30 — save it for Thursday.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 1: hips, glutes, calves, single-leg work (30–40 min)"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "Reps: 7:55–8:05/mi, HR upper Z3 / low Z4", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi total: 1.5 mi WU @ 9:45/mi → 5 × 1 mi @ goal MP (7:55–8:05/mi) with 90 sec easy jog recovery → 1.5 mi CD. Hilly route — hold effort, not pace, on any climb."}, "date": "2026-09-03", "notes": "Discipline session. First rep should feel easy — if it's under 7:50 you went too hot. Rhythm, not racing. Bail at 4 reps if form breaks down.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Fixed rest day (travel). Hydrate and eat normally — fueling for Sunday starts here.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:00/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi very easy, flat"}, "date": "2026-09-05", "notes": "Pre-long shakeout. Boring is good.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 2 (short, 20–25 min): core + calves"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "Easy segment 9:45–9:55/mi, HR ≤150; MP close 7:55–8:05/mi", "distanceM": 25750, "structure": "16 mi: first 13 mi easy (Z2, 9:45–9:55/mi), last 3 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi). Carry fluids with electrolytes; take a gel every 30–35 min starting around minute 35, with one extra ~15 min before the MP segment."}, "date": "2026-09-06", "notes": "Fueling rehearsal — dial in what sits well. If HR in the easy portion drifts above 155 and won't come back, skip the MP finish and just finish aerobic. Heat trumps pace every time.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W36"}	## Headline\nLast week landed exactly as planned: a lighter 33.7 mi after the 42.5 bump, long run cruised at 9:38/mi with HR behaving (148 avg, drift only +8.5 over 10.9 mi), and your body check reads "best I've felt in a month." That's the signal to climb again. This week we go to ~41 mi with a 16 mi long run and a marathon-pace workout on Thursday. You're 11 weeks out — the specific marathon-pace engine is the main thing we're building now.\n\n## Why these sessions\n- **Thursday quality — MP cruise intervals on the hilly route:** 5 × 1 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi) with 90 sec jog. One more rep than last block, same pace. Hills steal pace but build the strength your 3:30 goal needs. Run by effort on any climb.\n- **Sunday long run — 16 mi easy with a 3-mile MP finish:** Progresses +1 mi from your biggest long so far (15.2) and introduces running goal pace on fatigued legs. Start conservative (9:50/mi), then roll to ~8:00/mi for miles 14–16. If heat spikes HR above 155 on the easy portion, drop the MP finish and just keep moving.\n- **Easy days (Tue/Wed/Sat):** Protect the aerobic base. You're already drifting toward the fast end of easy (9:30/mi on Wed). Hold 9:35–9:50 — pace will come down naturally as fitness builds.\n\n## Your question — fueling and drinking on long runs\nAt 10+ miles / 90+ minutes, "going without" starts to cost you — late-run HR drift and sluggish last miles trace back to glycogen as much as heat. Baseline plan:\n\n- **Carbs:** Aim for **30–60 g carbs per hour** once you're past 60 minutes. Easiest form is a gel every 30–35 min (most gels are ~22–25 g). For a 2:30 long run that's roughly 3–4 gels. Take the first one around 30–40 min in, not when you already feel empty.\n- **Fluids:** **~16–24 oz per hour** in current heat, with electrolytes (tab in the bottle or a sports drink). Carry a handheld or belt — don't start thirsty.\n- **Pre-run:** A small carb snack 30–60 min before (banana, toast + honey, half a bagel) — ~30–50 g carbs. Coffee if you want it.\n- **MP finishes especially:** Take a gel 10–15 min before the MP segment so the sugar is on board when you need it.\n\nTreat long runs as fueling rehearsal — whatever you plan to use on Nov 15, practice it now. Report back what sits well.\n\n## Flags\n- Sleep bumped back to 7.4 hr — keep that trending. It's the single best thing supporting the ramp.\n- Shin history: nothing's whispering, but if the mileage bump brings any dull ache along the shin, tell me immediately — we back off before it becomes a problem, not after.\n- Thursday's reps last block hit 6:34–6:41/mi, which is well under goal MP. That's half-marathon pace territory, not marathon. This week lock onto 7:55–8:05/mi — the discipline is the workout.	\N	2026-08-30 20:00:43.098013+00	2026-08-30 20:00:43.098013+00	\N
77bbe22a-17ad-41ee-9fa5-7b4ffd625118	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-07", "notes": "Fixed rest/travel. 10 min mobility if you feel like it — ankles, hips, calves.", "dayType": "rest", "strength": "Optional mobility (10 min)"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35–9:55/mi, Z2 (HR 138–148)", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi conversational on the canal path"}, "date": "2026-09-08", "notes": "Shake-out after Sunday's effort. Genuinely easy — no pushing even if legs feel bouncy.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:25–9:45/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 14484, "structure": "9 mi easy + 6×20s strides on flat with full walk recovery after the run"}, "date": "2026-09-09", "notes": "Midweek aerobic session. Strides are relaxed turnover — not sprints.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 1 (30–40 min): hips, glutes, calves, single-leg work"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP reps 7:55–8:05/mi; WU/CD 9:40–10:00/mi", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi total on hilly route. 2 mi warm-up → 2 × 2 mi @ goal MP (8:00/mi) with 3:00 easy jog between → 1.5 mi cool-down"}, "date": "2026-09-10", "notes": "Step up from 1-mile reps to 2-mile reps. First rep should feel controlled, not racing — if mile 1 is already under 7:50, pull back. On hills: hold effort, let pace drift 5–10 sec.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-11", "notes": "Fixed rest. Hydrate and eat normally — fueling for Sunday starts today.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi very easy on flat canal path"}, "date": "2026-09-12", "notes": "Pre-long flush. Slower is better.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 2 (20–25 min): core + calves. Skip if legs are flat."}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "First 14 mi: HR ≤150, pace likely 9:30–9:50/mi. Last 3 mi: 7:55–8:05/mi (goal MP)", "distanceM": 27359, "structure": "17 mi: 14 mi easy/steady by HR, then 3 mi at goal MP to finish. Bail on the MP close if HR is already past 160 at mile 14 — heat and fatigue trump the pace target."}, "date": "2026-09-13", "notes": "First 14 mi HR-capped at 150 — if HR drifts above, walk 30s and resume slower. Then roll into 8:00/mi for the last 3. Start pre-sunrise if warm. Fuel: gel every 30–35 min (4 gels total), small water chase. Note how your gut handles each one.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W37"}	## Headline\nBreakthrough week. 44.4 mi, long run out to 16.1 with a sharp MP finish (back half 9:07 vs front 9:25), and you said marathon pace felt controlled for the first time. That's the switch flipping. HR behavior on easy days is unchanged (144, rock steady) even as easy pace keeps improving — your aerobic engine is where we want it ~10 weeks out.\n\nWe're going to lean into it this week: hold volume around 45 mi, push the long run to 17 with a short MP finish, and do a stronger MP interval session on Thursday. Nothing exotic — just more of what's working.\n\n## Flags / things I'm watching\n- **Long-run HR drift Sunday: +13.4 bpm (144 → 157)**, above the ≤10 target. Front end was honest Z2 but the two MP miles at the end plus heat pushed drift up. That's acceptable *because* of the MP close, but it means I don't want to chase both big distance AND a long MP finish in the same week yet. This week: more distance, shorter MP close.\n- **Z1+Z2 at 74.6%** — a touch below the 80% base target. Driven by the MP run and the long-run finish, not by easy days creeping. Keep Tue/Wed/Sat genuinely easy and we're fine.\n- **Mileage jump of +10.7 mi over the week prior**. We don't want to stack another big jump on top; this week holds roughly flat.\n- Shin history noted — nothing bothering you, but if anything whispers, tell me early.\n\n## Your question — gels on the long run\nYes, start now. You're 10 weeks out and race-day fueling is a skill you have to practice. Rules of thumb:\n- Take a gel **every 30–35 minutes**, starting around minute 30. On a 17-miler that's roughly 4 gels.\n- Chase with a few sips of water (not sports drink with the gel — too much sugar at once can backfire).\n- Pick one or two brands and stick with them through the block so race day is boring and predictable.\n- Target ~30–60 g of carbs per hour this block; we can push toward 60–80 g/hr closer to race day if your gut tolerates it.\n- If you get GI distress, note which gel and we adjust.\n\n## Why each session\n- **Tue easy 6**: shake-out, Z2, flat canal. Nothing to prove.\n- **Wed easy 9 + strides**: your bread-and-butter midweek aerobic session. 6×20s strides after keep leg speed alive without cost.\n- **Thu quality — 2 × 2 mi @ MP**: step up from last block's 1-mile reps. Longer continuous time at 8:00/mi so the pace stops feeling like a workout and starts feeling like cruising. Hilly route is fine; hold effort on climbs.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 17 with 3 mi MP finish**: +1 mi distance, slightly longer MP close than last week. HR-cap the first 14 (≤150), then roll into 8:00/mi for the last 3. Fuel plan above.\n\nTotal ~45 mi.	\N	2026-09-06 20:00:44.169072+00	2026-09-06 20:00:44.169072+00	\N
0876b9b6-d823-4b3e-97fb-b670672066db	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-14", "notes": "Fixed rest. Gentle calf and ankle mobility is fine — no aggressive stretching of the Achilles, no eccentric heel drops this week unless a clinician has prescribed them.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40–10:00/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "5-min brisk walk warm-up, then 4 mi very easy on the flat canal path. No strides. No pickups."}, "date": "2026-09-15", "notes": "This is the test run. If the Achilles stays ≤3/10 and quiets as you go, finish the 4. If it sharpens or alters your stride, walk home and we treat Wednesday as rest.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40–10:00/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5-min walk warm-up, then 5 mi flat canal path, conversational."}, "date": "2026-09-16", "notes": "Only run this if Tuesday was clean. If any pain lingered after Tuesday, swap today for rest or 30 min easy cross-train (bike/elliptical, no running).", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Short core + hip session, 20 min. Skip any heavy calf loading this week."}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "5-min walk warm-up, then 4 mi flat and easy. Not the hilly route — flat only."}, "date": "2026-09-17", "notes": "This replaces the tempo. No pace work, no hills. If the Achilles has been quiet all week this is just an easy aerobic day; if it's grumbling, make it 30 min easy cross-train instead and don't force the run.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"date": "2026-09-18", "notes": "Fixed rest. Normal eating, hydrate. Use the day to honestly check in with how the tendon feels on stairs and first steps in the morning — that's useful info.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, HR Z2", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "5-min walk warm-up, then 4 mi flat and easy."}, "date": "2026-09-19", "notes": "Pre-long shakeout. If anything felt off Thursday, cut this to 3 mi or skip.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:00/mi, HR ≤150", "distanceM": 14484, "structure": "9 mi flat and easy, out-and-back on the canal path. No MP work, no fast finish. Walk 30s at mile 3 and mile 6 as a check-in — how does the Achilles feel? If any sharpness, cut it short and walk back."}, "date": "2026-09-20", "notes": "Down from 10 last week on purpose. Keep practicing fueling — gel every 30–35 min, fluids with electrolytes. This is a feel-it-out long run, not a progression.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W38"}	## Headline\nWe're pulling back this week. The Achilles signal on Thursday — tight from mile 2, sharper when you picked up the pace, enough to stop and walk home — is exactly the kind of thing we treat conservatively right now, not push through. You still got a solid 10-miler in on Sunday with no pain while running, which is a good sign, but stiffness afterward tells me the tissue is irritated. We lose nothing by taking 7–10 easy days here. We lose the whole block if we poke it.\n\nPlan this week: no tempo, no MP work, no hills, all flat, all Ghost 16. Shorter easy runs, a modest long run capped by feel, and a hard stop rule if symptoms escalate. We're 9 weeks from Lakeshore — plenty of runway to rebuild quality if the Achilles settles.\n\n## Your questions\n\n**"Should I stop running for a few days, or keep going with easy runs?"**\nIf pain is ≤3/10, not sharp, and eases within the first mile (like Saturday), easy running on flats is usually fine and can actually help. If it's sharp, worsens as you run, changes your gait, or lingers/worsens after — stop that day and take 2–3 full days off. The tempo-day pattern (fine easy, bad with pace) is the key signal: speed is off the menu until it's quiet for a full week.\n\n**"How do I know if this is serious?"**\nI'm a coach, not a clinician, so I can't diagnose this — and I won't try. What I can tell you is which signs mean "see a sports physio or sports medicine doc this week, don't wait": sharp or stabbing pain, pain that worsens run-over-run, morning pain in the first steps out of bed that doesn't ease, visible swelling or a lump, pain squeezing the tendon, or any change in how your foot pushes off. If any of those show up, please book an appointment — early assessment on Achilles stuff is cheap insurance. See the callout below.\n\n## Flags\n- Tempo Thursday: tightness → sharper with pace → stopped at 3 mi. Clear signal from the tissue.\n- Sunday long run was pain-free running but stiff after — tendon is grumbling, not screaming. That's the window to be smart.\n- Mileage already came off (31 vs 44) — don't rush it back. Reload gradually once symptoms are quiet.\n- Sleep 7 hrs, stress moderate, anxious about the block — I hear you. One cautious week does not throw away a marathon. Grinding on an angry Achilles can.\n- Shoes: all easy miles in Ghost 16. Keep the Endorphin Speed 4 in the closet until we're back doing workouts.\n\n## How to read this week\n- Each run, do a 5-minute walk warm-up before you start running. Flat routes only — the hilly Thursday loop is out.\n- Pain rules every day: ≤3/10 and not sharp = continue; sharp, worsening, or altering your gait = stop and walk home. Report back what you felt in the check-in.\n- No strides, no pickups, no hills, no MP touches. Pure easy aerobic.\n- If two consecutive runs hurt more than the one before, stop running and get it looked at before the next session.	\N	2026-09-13 20:00:54.06104+00	2026-09-13 20:00:54.06104+00	[{"body": "I can coach around a grumbling tendon, but I can't diagnose it — and Achilles issues are worth catching early. Please book a sports physio or sports medicine appointment this week if you notice any of: sharp or stabbing pain while running, pain that worsens run-over-run, pain or stiffness in the first steps out of bed that doesn't ease within a few minutes, visible swelling or a lump on the tendon, tenderness when you pinch the tendon between thumb and finger, or any change in how your foot pushes off. Early assessment is cheap insurance for the marathon block.", "title": "Get the Achilles looked at if these signs show up", "severity": "caution"}, {"body": "During any run, stop and walk home if: pain goes above 3/10, becomes sharp, worsens as you run, or changes your stride. One or two cautious days won't cost you the race. Pushing through an angry tendon can. Tell me in your next check-in exactly what you felt and when — that detail shapes next week's plan.", "title": "Stop-the-run rules this week", "severity": "info"}]
c6dde52e-1142-4497-87f4-54e4f9dc43af	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-21", "notes": "Fixed rest/travel. Slow heel drops morning and evening as prescribed. 10 min mobility optional.", "dayType": "rest", "strength": "Heel drops (physio protocol) + optional mobility"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35–9:55/mi, Z2 (HR 138–148)", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi conversational on flat canal path"}, "date": "2026-09-22", "notes": "Shake-out. Properly easy — if the Achilles is stiff for the first mile that's expected, but it should ease. If it sharpens, cut short.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:25–9:45/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi easy, flat"}, "date": "2026-09-23", "notes": "Primary aerobic day. No strides this week — one more week of boring miles while the tendon settles.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Heel drops AM/PM + strength 1 (30–40 min): hips, glutes, calves, single-leg"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "Tempo 7:55–8:05/mi; WU/CD 9:40–10:00/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi on flat route (not the hilly one): 1.5 mi WU → 2 mi continuous @ goal MP (8:00/mi) → 1.5 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-09-24", "notes": "Pre-conditions: no morning Achilles stiffness, no sharpening pain in warm-up. If either, convert the tempo portion to easy and run 5 mi Z2. During the 2-mile tempo, if anything sharpens in the tendon, stop and jog home.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "notes": "Fixed rest. Heel drops. Fueling for Sunday starts today — eat and hydrate normally.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi very easy on flat canal"}, "date": "2026-09-26", "notes": "Pre-long flush. Slower is better.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Heel drops + short strength 2 (20–25 min): core + calves"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "HR ≤150, pace likely 9:30–9:50/mi", "distanceM": 17703, "structure": "11 mi steady by HR on flat/canal route"}, "date": "2026-09-27", "notes": "HR-capped long run — no MP finish this week. If HR drifts above 150, walk 30s and resume slower. Gel every 30–35 min (2 gels) with small water chase to keep practicing fueling. Report back how the Achilles handles the extra time on feet.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W39"}	## Headline\nGood news dominates: the Achilles backed down, Sunday's long run was pain-free with no afterward stiffness, and your physio has cleared you to keep running — including one short tempo this week if morning stiffness stays gone. Easy pace and HR behavior are unchanged (143 avg, 91% Z1+Z2), which tells me the aerobic engine held together through the quiet week. We're going to rebuild carefully: ~32 mi, a short controlled tempo on Thursday, and a long run back out to 11 miles.\n\n## Your questions\n**1. Can we put a short tempo in?** Yes — exactly as your physio framed it. Thursday is a short, honest tempo (2 miles continuous at ~8:00/mi, your goal MP), not a hammer session. The pre-conditions are the ones she set: no morning stiffness in the Achilles the day of the run, and no sharpening pain during. If either shows up, we convert to easy and move on. No heroics.\n\n**2. Does the missed week change what's realistic on Nov 15?** Honestly: 3:30 is still on the table but it's tighter now. You lost one quality week and shortened a long run, not a block. What matters most now is stacking 3–4 clean weeks of progression (long runs back to 15, 17, then 18–19) with MP work mixed in. I'd rather commit to a goal after your next long run at 13–14 mi tells us how the Achilles handles the extra time on feet. Right now the plan is still built around 3:30 — we'll calibrate honestly in 2–3 weeks.\n\n## Why each session\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out, flat canal, Z2. Nothing to prove.\n- **Wed easy 6**: aerobic volume. No strides this week — leg speed can wait one more week while the Achilles settles further.\n- **Thu short tempo**: 1.5 mi WU → 2 mi @ ~8:00/mi → 1.5 mi CD. Flat route, not the hillier one, to keep load off the tendon. Workout shoes OK; if the Achilles feels anything other than normal first-mile stiffness, downshift to easy.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush, slower end of easy.\n- **Sun long 11**: +3 mi from last Sunday, HR-capped at 150. No MP finish this week — we're earning volume back before adding intensity to the long run.\n\n## Flags\n- Keep up the slow heel drops twice a day as prescribed — that's the single most important thing you're doing right now.\n- Morning stiffness is your canary. If it reappears for more than the first few steps, email me before Thursday's workout and we'll pull it.\n- Mileage ~32 mi — a ~6 mi bump from last week. Fine on paper, but your tendon is the governor, not the number.	\N	2026-09-20 20:00:44.509429+00	2026-09-20 20:00:44.509429+00	[{"body": "You've been cleared to keep running with one short tempo, which is what the plan reflects. If morning stiffness returns, if pain sharpens during a run, or if the tendon feels worse day-over-day, stop the session and check back in with your physio before the next hard day. I can coach the training around it, but anything that feels like a step backward is a conversation for her, not me.", "title": "Keep your physio in the loop on the Achilles", "severity": "caution"}]
6de0ee3e-ff03-4b5c-a998-09eedbead69f	{"days": [{"date": "2026-10-05", "notes": "Fixed rest/travel. Optional 10 min mobility — ankles, calves, hips. Hydrate well after Sunday's long effort.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30–9:50/mi, Z2 (HR 138–148)", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi conversational on flat canal path"}, "date": "2026-10-06", "notes": "Shake-out after Sunday's 17 w/ MP finish. Genuinely easy — if HR won't settle under 150, slow down. No pushing.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30–9:50/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi easy on flat canal + 6×20s relaxed strides after the run with full walk recovery"}, "date": "2026-10-07", "notes": "Primary aerobic day. Strides are relaxed turnover, not sprints — keeps leg speed alive for Thursday.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Strength session 1 (30–40 min): hips, glutes, calves, single-leg work"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP reps 7:55–8:05/mi; WU/CD 9:40–10:00/mi", "distanceM": 14484, "structure": "9 mi total on hilly route: 1.5 mi WU → 3 × 2 mi @ goal MP (8:00/mi) with 3:00 easy jog between → 1.5 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-10-08", "notes": "Step up from one 2-mi block to three. Rep 1 should feel controlled — if mile 1 is under 7:50 you went too hot, dial back. On climbs: hold effort, let pace drift 5–10s. Bail at 2 reps if form breaks down or legs are flat.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "notes": "Fixed rest/travel. Hydrate and eat normally — fueling for Sunday starts today.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45–10:05/mi, Z2", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi very easy on flat canal"}, "date": "2026-10-10", "notes": "Pre-long flush. Slower is better. If legs feel heavy from Thursday, cut to 4 mi and move on.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Short strength 2 (20–25 min): core + calves. Skip if legs are flat."}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "HR ≤150, pace likely 9:30–9:50/mi", "distanceM": 24140, "structure": "15 mi steady by HR on flat/canal route. No MP finish this week."}, "date": "2026-10-11", "notes": "Deliberate step back from last Sunday's 17 w/ MP — we're protecting legs for the 19–20 mi peak long run next week. If HR drifts above 150, walk 30s and resume slower. Fuel: gel every 30–35 min (3 gels), small water chase, carry electrolytes. Report back how HR drift looks and how fueling sat.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W41"}	## Headline\nThat was a big, honest week — 44 mi with a 17.2 mi long run that finished with 3 miles at marathon pace, and you came through it saying "I could have held it." That's exactly the feedback I wanted. The aerobic engine is doing its job: easy HR still parked at 144, easy pace trending from 9:23 to 9:14 across the month, and MP felt like a controlled gear rather than a strain.\n\nThat said, two things on the dashboard tell me we don't double down this week — we consolidate:\n\n1. **Long-run drift was +15.9 bpm** (target ≤10). The front half was fine, but your HR climbed from 144 to 160 across the back end, pushed by the MP close on tired legs. That's acceptable *because* of the MP finish, but it's a cost.\n2. **Relative effort 317 vs 223 three-week average** — a one-week overload flag. Fine on its own; a problem if we stack another big jump on top.\n\nAdd in that you went from 35 mi to 44 mi in one jump, and the shin history in the back of my mind, and the right move is a slight pullback this week: hold volume roughly flat (~42 mi), keep the Thursday MP work (step it up a notch), and bring the long run down to 15 mi with no MP finish — pure aerobic, HR-capped. Then next week we push again.\n\n## Your question — how the final weeks look\n\nYou're 6 weeks out from Lakeshore after this one. Here's the shape:\n\n- **This week (Oct 5–11, 6 weeks out)** — consolidate. ~42 mi, long 15 easy, Thursday 3×2 mi @ MP.\n- **Oct 12–18 (5 weeks out)** — peak long run. ~46 mi, long 19–20 mi with a 4–5 mi MP block in the back half. This is the key workout of the block.\n- **Oct 19–25 (4 weeks out)** — biggest MP volume week. ~45 mi, long 17–18 mi with 6–8 mi at MP, plus a strong Thursday quality.\n- **Oct 26–Nov 1 (3 weeks out)** — taper begins. ~36 mi, long 14 mi easy with a short MP touch, Thursday sharpens (shorter, race-pace focused).\n- **Nov 2–8 (2 weeks out)** — deeper taper. ~28 mi, long 10–11 mi, one short MP session midweek.\n- **Nov 9–15 (race week)** — ~18–20 mi total including the race. Short shake-outs, a few MP strides on Thursday, race Sunday.\n\nThe engine's built. From here it's three more progression weeks, then three weeks of carefully protecting what you've got.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 6**: shake out from Sunday. Nothing to prove — if HR won't drop under 150, slow down.\n- **Wed easy 8 + strides**: midweek aerobic volume + 6×20s to keep leg speed sharp without cost. Flat canal path.\n- **Thu quality — 3×2 mi @ MP**: step up from last week's single 2-mile block to three. 6 total miles at 8:00/mi is where we start living. Hilly route is fine; hold effort on climbs.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 15, HR ≤150, no MP finish**: deliberate step back after last Sunday's 17 w/ MP. Pure aerobic, fuel practice, protect the legs for the 19–20 mi peak long run the following week.\n\n## Flags\n- Last week's HR drift and overload flag are why this week consolidates. Not a problem — a signal to pace the build.\n- Keep Tue/Wed/Sat genuinely easy. 9:14 is drifting toward the fast end of easy; aim 9:30–9:50. The engine builds while you resist running fast on easy days.\n- Shin history is quiet. Report any whisper early, not after.\n- Fueling on Sunday: still practice — 2–3 gels across a 15-miler plus electrolytes. The gut needs the reps too.	\N	2026-10-04 20:01:12.695854+00	2026-10-04 20:01:12.695854+00	\N
