146acec0-d2e4-4e4e-973f-aed33b284730	coach	## Headline\nLast week landed exactly as planned: a lighter 33.7 mi after the 42.5 bump, long run cruised at 9:38/mi with HR behaving (148 avg, drift only +8.5 over 10.9 mi), and your body check reads "best I've felt in a month." That's the signal to climb again. This week we go to ~41 mi with a 16 mi long run and a marathon-pace workout on Thursday. You're 11 weeks out — the specific marathon-pace engine is the main thing we're building now.\n\n## Why these sessions\n- **Thursday quality — MP cruise intervals on the hilly route:** 5 × 1 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi) with 90 sec jog. One more rep than last block, same pace. Hills steal pace but build the strength your 3:30 goal needs. Run by effort on any climb.\n- **Sunday long run — 16 mi easy with a 3-mile MP finish:** Progresses +1 mi from your biggest long so far (15.2) and introduces running goal pace on fatigued legs. Start conservative (9:50/mi), then roll to ~8:00/mi for miles 14–16. If heat spikes HR above 155 on the easy portion, drop the MP finish and just keep moving.\n- **Easy days (Tue/Wed/Sat):** Protect the aerobic base. You're already drifting toward the fast end of easy (9:30/mi on Wed). Hold 9:35–9:50 — pace will come down naturally as fitness builds.\n\n## Your question — fueling and drinking on long runs\nAt 10+ miles / 90+ minutes, "going without" starts to cost you — late-run HR drift and sluggish last miles trace back to glycogen as much as heat. Baseline plan:\n\n- **Carbs:** Aim for **30–60 g carbs per hour** once you're past 60 minutes. Easiest form is a gel every 30–35 min (most gels are ~22–25 g). For a 2:30 long run that's roughly 3–4 gels. Take the first one around 30–40 min in, not when you already feel empty.\n- **Fluids:** **~16–24 oz per hour** in current heat, with electrolytes (tab in the bottle or a sports drink). Carry a handheld or belt — don't start thirsty.\n- **Pre-run:** A small carb snack 30–60 min before (banana, toast + honey, half a bagel) — ~30–50 g carbs. Coffee if you want it.\n- **MP finishes especially:** Take a gel 10–15 min before the MP segment so the sugar is on board when you need it.\n\nTreat long runs as fueling rehearsal — whatever you plan to use on Nov 15, practice it now. Report back what sits well.\n\n## Flags\n- Sleep bumped back to 7.4 hr — keep that trending. It's the single best thing supporting the ramp.\n- Shin history: nothing's whispering, but if the mileage bump brings any dull ache along the shin, tell me immediately — we back off before it becomes a problem, not after.\n- Thursday's reps last block hit 6:34–6:41/mi, which is well under goal MP. That's half-marathon pace territory, not marathon. This week lock onto 7:55–8:05/mi — the discipline is the workout.	2026-08-30 20:00:43.09969+00	cc5d18d4-c308-481b-b011-dd3c99e03ba0	\N	1856dd82-57b5-47af-9e36-e00a2b21cddb	\N	\N
a034eaa7-af2d-4283-9768-fcb5627f2ce6	coach	## Headline\nBreakthrough week. 44.4 mi, long run out to 16.1 with a sharp MP finish (back half 9:07 vs front 9:25), and you said marathon pace felt controlled for the first time. That's the switch flipping. HR behavior on easy days is unchanged (144, rock steady) even as easy pace keeps improving — your aerobic engine is where we want it ~10 weeks out.\n\nWe're going to lean into it this week: hold volume around 45 mi, push the long run to 17 with a short MP finish, and do a stronger MP interval session on Thursday. Nothing exotic — just more of what's working.\n\n## Flags / things I'm watching\n- **Long-run HR drift Sunday: +13.4 bpm (144 → 157)**, above the ≤10 target. Front end was honest Z2 but the two MP miles at the end plus heat pushed drift up. That's acceptable *because* of the MP close, but it means I don't want to chase both big distance AND a long MP finish in the same week yet. This week: more distance, shorter MP close.\n- **Z1+Z2 at 74.6%** — a touch below the 80% base target. Driven by the MP run and the long-run finish, not by easy days creeping. Keep Tue/Wed/Sat genuinely easy and we're fine.\n- **Mileage jump of +10.7 mi over the week prior**. We don't want to stack another big jump on top; this week holds roughly flat.\n- Shin history noted — nothing bothering you, but if anything whispers, tell me early.\n\n## Your question — gels on the long run\nYes, start now. You're 10 weeks out and race-day fueling is a skill you have to practice. Rules of thumb:\n- Take a gel **every 30–35 minutes**, starting around minute 30. On a 17-miler that's roughly 4 gels.\n- Chase with a few sips of water (not sports drink with the gel — too much sugar at once can backfire).\n- Pick one or two brands and stick with them through the block so race day is boring and predictable.\n- Target ~30–60 g of carbs per hour this block; we can push toward 60–80 g/hr closer to race day if your gut tolerates it.\n- If you get GI distress, note which gel and we adjust.\n\n## Why each session\n- **Tue easy 6**: shake-out, Z2, flat canal. Nothing to prove.\n- **Wed easy 9 + strides**: your bread-and-butter midweek aerobic session. 6×20s strides after keep leg speed alive without cost.\n- **Thu quality — 2 × 2 mi @ MP**: step up from last block's 1-mile reps. Longer continuous time at 8:00/mi so the pace stops feeling like a workout and starts feeling like cruising. Hilly route is fine; hold effort on climbs.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 17 with 3 mi MP finish**: +1 mi distance, slightly longer MP close than last week. HR-cap the first 14 (≤150), then roll into 8:00/mi for the last 3. Fuel plan above.\n\nTotal ~45 mi.	2026-09-06 20:00:44.17077+00	140f5949-e890-44f8-9296-6ffd441831a4	\N	f17efaf4-967e-4ff8-a15f-257d9b247dc2	\N	\N
