bdbc1093-a4a3-44b3-bb6a-5a7fe7d34a87	coach	## Headline\nYou're building nicely — 14 weeks out, 37.7 mi last week, long run up to 13 mi at 9:41/mi with HR behaving (144 easy, no upward trend). This week we hold mileage roughly flat (~38 mi), swap the 800s for marathon-pace work (addresses your concern that reps went too hot early), and push the long run to 14 mi by effort, not pace.\n\n## Why these sessions\n- **Thursday quality — MP intervals on the hilly route**: You flagged the 800s felt too hot early. At 14 weeks out we don't need pure VO2; we need the marathon-pace engine. 4 × 1 mi @ ~8:00/mi (your 3:30 target pace) teaches pacing discipline and rhythm with low injury cost. Hills give strength without the pounding of flat speed.\n- **Sunday long run — 14 mi easy**: Progressing +1 mi from last week. Keep it a conversational Z2 effort. In heat, HR tells the truth and pace lies.\n- **Easy days stay easy**: Your Z1+Z2 was 81% last week — right on target. Keep Tue/Wed/Sat honest at 9:40–9:50/mi so Thursday and Sunday can land.\n\n## Answer to your question\n**Should I slow the long run in the heat?** Yes — run by effort/HR, not pace. In 25°C+ at 6 am, expect to add 15–30 sec/mi versus a cool day to keep HR in Z2 (131–150). If HR drifts above 155 and won't come back, walk 30 sec at aid stops or cut it short. Pace will come back when the weather does; aerobic time on feet is what we're banking.\n\n## Flags\n- Last week's relative effort (304) is climbing fast — three straight weeks of +4 mi. We hold mileage flat this week to let adaptations catch up before the next bump.\n- Sleep averaging 6.9 hr is on the low side. If you can nudge toward 7.5 on quality-day eves, Thursday will feel better.\n- Shin history noted — nothing bothering you now, but if anything starts whispering, tell me early.	2026-08-16 20:00:36.223059+00	89597b69-9acd-416c-a37d-3865024c449b	\N	fb868588-b0bd-45f7-b5ee-7eccb8290bb6	2	\N
05684d22-2c07-4b17-b15a-8cb22d2bb039	coach	## Headline\nBig week — 42.5 mi, long run out to 15.2, and still 82% in Z1+Z2. Impressive work, especially stacked on top of a tough stretch at work (late nights, 6.4 hrs sleep, Saturday felt like a slog). But that's also a +7 mi jump over your 3-week average and relative effort climbed from 272 to 342. You asked for an easier week — you've earned it, and the training calls for it. This week of Aug 24–30 is a planned cutback: ~36 mi, long run back to 12, and one marathon-pace session that's crisp but short. We'll push again next week from a fresher starting point.\n\n## Flags / things I'm watching\n- **Load spike + low sleep + high stress**: classic combo that precedes a niggle, especially with your shin-splint history. Nothing hurts yet, so we take the cutback *now* rather than after a problem shows up.\n- **Long-run HR drift**: 144 → 151 over 15 miles on Sunday is on the higher side — the heat is biting. Keep the HR cap of 150 on long runs for now; let pace fall where it does.\n- **Thursday tempo**: 7:39–7:47/mi is faster than goal MP (8:00/mi). You have the top-end gear; this week I want you back on honest MP so the pace feels automatic.\n- **Strength**: 1 of 2 again. On a down week with less fatigue, two short sessions are very doable.\n\n## Your question — handling missed runs when you travel\nSimple rules, in priority order:\n1. **Never skip the long run** if you can help it. If travel kills Sunday, move it to Saturday or do it on the road the day you fly.\n2. **Protect one quality session per week**. If the Thursday workout is at risk, shift it to Wednesday or Tuesday and make the other day easy.\n3. **Easy miles are the most droppable.** If you lose 1–2 easy days to travel, don't try to cram them in — just land on the plane rested and resume.\n4. **Don't stack a missed long run onto the next week.** Pick up where the plan is, don't try to "make up" miles — that's how injuries happen.\n5. Give me a heads-up on travel weeks in your check-in and I'll shape the plan around the fixed slots you'll have.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out, pure Z2.\n- **Wed easy 7 + strides**: aerobic volume plus 6×20s strides for leg turnover without stress.\n- **Thu quality — 3 × 1 mi at MP**: shorter rep set on the hilly route. Goal is to groove 8:00/mi, not to grind.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 12, HR-capped**: distance comes down intentionally. Run by HR (≤150), let pace be whatever it is. No MP finish this week — the point is recovery adaptation.\n\nTotal ~36 mi (down from 42.5). Next week we rebuild toward ~40 with an MP long run.	2026-08-23 20:00:51.341511+00	673db0ec-b22b-4732-9a14-3b48998783a8	\N	ed340751-9120-4422-9dd6-419df84a6980	\N	[{"body": "You've had a few weeks of climbing mileage, lower sleep (6.4 hrs avg), and high work stress. Nothing is hurting now, which is the right time to pull back — not after. If you notice shin tenderness, aching along the bone, or any pain that worsens during or after a run, pause running and check in with a sports physio or doctor rather than training through it. Don't try to make up this week's miles later.", "title": "Load + sleep combo — watch the shins", "severity": "caution"}]
c6553f96-e8b8-4fb2-9e5f-89bdfe0442f4	coach	## Headline\nWe're pulling back this week. The Achilles signal on Thursday — tight from mile 2, sharper when you picked up the pace, enough to stop and walk home — is exactly the kind of thing we treat conservatively right now, not push through. You still got a solid 10-miler in on Sunday with no pain while running, which is a good sign, but stiffness afterward tells me the tissue is irritated. We lose nothing by taking 7–10 easy days here. We lose the whole block if we poke it.\n\nPlan this week: no tempo, no MP work, no hills, all flat, all Ghost 16. Shorter easy runs, a modest long run capped by feel, and a hard stop rule if symptoms escalate. We're 9 weeks from Lakeshore — plenty of runway to rebuild quality if the Achilles settles.\n\n## Your questions\n\n**"Should I stop running for a few days, or keep going with easy runs?"**\nIf pain is ≤3/10, not sharp, and eases within the first mile (like Saturday), easy running on flats is usually fine and can actually help. If it's sharp, worsens as you run, changes your gait, or lingers/worsens after — stop that day and take 2–3 full days off. The tempo-day pattern (fine easy, bad with pace) is the key signal: speed is off the menu until it's quiet for a full week.\n\n**"How do I know if this is serious?"**\nI'm a coach, not a clinician, so I can't diagnose this — and I won't try. What I can tell you is which signs mean "see a sports physio or sports medicine doc this week, don't wait": sharp or stabbing pain, pain that worsens run-over-run, morning pain in the first steps out of bed that doesn't ease, visible swelling or a lump, pain squeezing the tendon, or any change in how your foot pushes off. If any of those show up, please book an appointment — early assessment on Achilles stuff is cheap insurance. See the callout below.\n\n## Flags\n- Tempo Thursday: tightness → sharper with pace → stopped at 3 mi. Clear signal from the tissue.\n- Sunday long run was pain-free running but stiff after — tendon is grumbling, not screaming. That's the window to be smart.\n- Mileage already came off (31 vs 44) — don't rush it back. Reload gradually once symptoms are quiet.\n- Sleep 7 hrs, stress moderate, anxious about the block — I hear you. One cautious week does not throw away a marathon. Grinding on an angry Achilles can.\n- Shoes: all easy miles in Ghost 16. Keep the Endorphin Speed 4 in the closet until we're back doing workouts.\n\n## How to read this week\n- Each run, do a 5-minute walk warm-up before you start running. Flat routes only — the hilly Thursday loop is out.\n- Pain rules every day: ≤3/10 and not sharp = continue; sharp, worsening, or altering your gait = stop and walk home. Report back what you felt in the check-in.\n- No strides, no pickups, no hills, no MP touches. Pure easy aerobic.\n- If two consecutive runs hurt more than the one before, stop running and get it looked at before the next session.	2026-09-13 20:00:54.063021+00	77bbe22a-17ad-41ee-9fa5-7b4ffd625118	\N	20309533-2a97-4c2e-9b3d-8f7572f9fbe8	\N	[{"body": "I can coach around a grumbling tendon, but I can't diagnose it — and Achilles issues are worth catching early. Please book a sports physio or sports medicine appointment this week if you notice any of: sharp or stabbing pain while running, pain that worsens run-over-run, pain or stiffness in the first steps out of bed that doesn't ease within a few minutes, visible swelling or a lump on the tendon, tenderness when you pinch the tendon between thumb and finger, or any change in how your foot pushes off. Early assessment is cheap insurance for the marathon block.", "title": "Get the Achilles looked at if these signs show up", "severity": "caution"}, {"body": "During any run, stop and walk home if: pain goes above 3/10, becomes sharp, worsens as you run, or changes your stride. One or two cautious days won't cost you the race. Pushing through an angry tendon can. Tell me in your next check-in exactly what you felt and when — that detail shapes next week's plan.", "title": "Stop-the-run rules this week", "severity": "info"}]
7a9f3cf2-1d88-4fc9-a221-2e1fccc2503f	coach	## Headline\nGood news dominates: the Achilles backed down, Sunday's long run was pain-free with no afterward stiffness, and your physio has cleared you to keep running — including one short tempo this week if morning stiffness stays gone. Easy pace and HR behavior are unchanged (143 avg, 91% Z1+Z2), which tells me the aerobic engine held together through the quiet week. We're going to rebuild carefully: ~32 mi, a short controlled tempo on Thursday, and a long run back out to 11 miles.\n\n## Your questions\n**1. Can we put a short tempo in?** Yes — exactly as your physio framed it. Thursday is a short, honest tempo (2 miles continuous at ~8:00/mi, your goal MP), not a hammer session. The pre-conditions are the ones she set: no morning stiffness in the Achilles the day of the run, and no sharpening pain during. If either shows up, we convert to easy and move on. No heroics.\n\n**2. Does the missed week change what's realistic on Nov 15?** Honestly: 3:30 is still on the table but it's tighter now. You lost one quality week and shortened a long run, not a block. What matters most now is stacking 3–4 clean weeks of progression (long runs back to 15, 17, then 18–19) with MP work mixed in. I'd rather commit to a goal after your next long run at 13–14 mi tells us how the Achilles handles the extra time on feet. Right now the plan is still built around 3:30 — we'll calibrate honestly in 2–3 weeks.\n\n## Why each session\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out, flat canal, Z2. Nothing to prove.\n- **Wed easy 6**: aerobic volume. No strides this week — leg speed can wait one more week while the Achilles settles further.\n- **Thu short tempo**: 1.5 mi WU → 2 mi @ ~8:00/mi → 1.5 mi CD. Flat route, not the hillier one, to keep load off the tendon. Workout shoes OK; if the Achilles feels anything other than normal first-mile stiffness, downshift to easy.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush, slower end of easy.\n- **Sun long 11**: +3 mi from last Sunday, HR-capped at 150. No MP finish this week — we're earning volume back before adding intensity to the long run.\n\n## Flags\n- Keep up the slow heel drops twice a day as prescribed — that's the single most important thing you're doing right now.\n- Morning stiffness is your canary. If it reappears for more than the first few steps, email me before Thursday's workout and we'll pull it.\n- Mileage ~32 mi — a ~6 mi bump from last week. Fine on paper, but your tendon is the governor, not the number.	2026-09-20 20:00:44.512031+00	0876b9b6-d823-4b3e-97fb-b670672066db	\N	fdb17644-6fe1-4179-8dbb-9e47f9cb54e8	\N	[{"body": "You've been cleared to keep running with one short tempo, which is what the plan reflects. If morning stiffness returns, if pain sharpens during a run, or if the tendon feels worse day-over-day, stop the session and check back in with your physio before the next hard day. I can coach the training around it, but anything that feels like a step backward is a conversation for her, not me.", "title": "Keep your physio in the loop on the Achilles", "severity": "caution"}]
06a9379c-5dc0-4826-a2bb-3c7f0c94f9de	coach	## Headline\nClean, confident week. 34.8 mi, tempo went well with Achilles quiet (your own note: "kept checking… it was fine"), long run out to 11.9 with a mild negative split and HR drift back inside acceptable range (+8.8 over nearly 2 hrs). Z1+Z2 at 81.5% — right where I want it. You're healthy, hitting marathon-base targets, and 7 weeks from Lakeshore. Time to build again, carefully.\n\nThis week: ~38 mi, step the long run to 13.5, and reintroduce real marathon-pace work on Thursday — 2 × 2 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi). We're not going back to the aggressive block we had before the Achilles flare; we're layering MP volume gradually so the tendon stays happy.\n\n## Your question — Achilles maintenance through race day\nKeep doing what's clearly working, don't invent new stuff:\n- **Slow heel drops** daily — the physio's protocol is doing its job; keep it going right through race week. On the Monday of race week drop to once a day, and skip them the day before the race.\n- **5-minute walk warm-up before every run.** Boring, free, protective. Keep it in.\n- **First-steps-in-the-morning check** — that's your canary. If morning stiffness ever returns and doesn't ease within a few minutes, tell me in the check-in before your next run.\n- **Shoe rotation** — Ghost 16 for easy, Endorphin Speed 4 only on quality days. Don't let an easy run accidentally happen in the Speed 4.\n- **One rebuild rule I'm applying**: we add either distance OR intensity in a given week, not big jumps of both. This week it's a modest distance bump plus returning MP; next week we'll either hold intensity and push the long run, or hold distance and sharpen quality.\n\nIf anything whispers at you — sharper than a 2/10, lingers after runs, or alters your gait — back to the physio before pushing on. Keep her in the loop as we get closer to race day too.\n\n## Flags\n- Thursday tempo HR max hit 174 (upper Z4). Intended effort was tempo, not threshold — this week's MP reps should sit mid-Z3 to low Z4, not spike. If you see 170+ on an MP mile, you're pushing too hard.\n- Long-run drift still slightly on the high side (+8.8). Not alarming given back-half pickup, but keep the HR cap of ≤150 on long runs this week — pace will be whatever it is.\n- Mileage jump +8.9 mi over prior week was a bit sharp — this week we only add ~3 mi on top so the ramp isn't stacking.\n- Sleep 7.4, strength 2 of 2, stress low — all green. Protect that.	2026-09-27 20:00:39.085249+00	c6dde52e-1142-4497-87f4-54e4f9dc43af	\N	80b9223e-ec44-48d5-b007-4b1f1c616ff1	\N	\N
920c1675-c309-4d82-bced-35c3fd2c7577	coach	## Headline\nThat was a big, honest week — 44 mi with a 17.2 mi long run that finished with 3 miles at marathon pace, and you came through it saying "I could have held it." That's exactly the feedback I wanted. The aerobic engine is doing its job: easy HR still parked at 144, easy pace trending from 9:23 to 9:14 across the month, and MP felt like a controlled gear rather than a strain.\n\nThat said, two things on the dashboard tell me we don't double down this week — we consolidate:\n\n1. **Long-run drift was +15.9 bpm** (target ≤10). The front half was fine, but your HR climbed from 144 to 160 across the back end, pushed by the MP close on tired legs. That's acceptable *because* of the MP finish, but it's a cost.\n2. **Relative effort 317 vs 223 three-week average** — a one-week overload flag. Fine on its own; a problem if we stack another big jump on top.\n\nAdd in that you went from 35 mi to 44 mi in one jump, and the shin history in the back of my mind, and the right move is a slight pullback this week: hold volume roughly flat (~42 mi), keep the Thursday MP work (step it up a notch), and bring the long run down to 15 mi with no MP finish — pure aerobic, HR-capped. Then next week we push again.\n\n## Your question — how the final weeks look\n\nYou're 6 weeks out from Lakeshore after this one. Here's the shape:\n\n- **This week (Oct 5–11, 6 weeks out)** — consolidate. ~42 mi, long 15 easy, Thursday 3×2 mi @ MP.\n- **Oct 12–18 (5 weeks out)** — peak long run. ~46 mi, long 19–20 mi with a 4–5 mi MP block in the back half. This is the key workout of the block.\n- **Oct 19–25 (4 weeks out)** — biggest MP volume week. ~45 mi, long 17–18 mi with 6–8 mi at MP, plus a strong Thursday quality.\n- **Oct 26–Nov 1 (3 weeks out)** — taper begins. ~36 mi, long 14 mi easy with a short MP touch, Thursday sharpens (shorter, race-pace focused).\n- **Nov 2–8 (2 weeks out)** — deeper taper. ~28 mi, long 10–11 mi, one short MP session midweek.\n- **Nov 9–15 (race week)** — ~18–20 mi total including the race. Short shake-outs, a few MP strides on Thursday, race Sunday.\n\nThe engine's built. From here it's three more progression weeks, then three weeks of carefully protecting what you've got.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 6**: shake out from Sunday. Nothing to prove — if HR won't drop under 150, slow down.\n- **Wed easy 8 + strides**: midweek aerobic volume + 6×20s to keep leg speed sharp without cost. Flat canal path.\n- **Thu quality — 3×2 mi @ MP**: step up from last week's single 2-mile block to three. 6 total miles at 8:00/mi is where we start living. Hilly route is fine; hold effort on climbs.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long flush.\n- **Sun long 15, HR ≤150, no MP finish**: deliberate step back after last Sunday's 17 w/ MP. Pure aerobic, fuel practice, protect the legs for the 19–20 mi peak long run the following week.\n\n## Flags\n- Last week's HR drift and overload flag are why this week consolidates. Not a problem — a signal to pace the build.\n- Keep Tue/Wed/Sat genuinely easy. 9:14 is drifting toward the fast end of easy; aim 9:30–9:50. The engine builds while you resist running fast on easy days.\n- Shin history is quiet. Report any whisper early, not after.\n- Fueling on Sunday: still practice — 2–3 gels across a 15-miler plus electrolytes. The gut needs the reps too.	2026-10-04 20:01:12.697619+00	42d956f4-8b0a-4145-98ff-a058741c4904	\N	4595506a-fb47-4876-90f9-fae6444a2d84	\N	\N
3d578573-67c2-44d8-b1c4-4f0c1037d56b	coach	## Headline\nBig week in the books — 45 miles, your longest run in two years, and a long run with a genuine negative split (back half 8:45/mi vs front 9:13/mi). That's a confidence builder. The 3:30 target is still live. But two things from this week tell me we pull the reins a little this week before we go hard again: your Thursday session went off-script into VO2 territory (max HR 184, 24% of the run in Z5), and the Sunday drift was +16.9 bpm — well above the +10 target. Combine that with sleep slipping to 6.8 hrs, legs heavy into Monday, and relative effort 1.3× your three-week average, and the smart play is a modest step-back now so we can hit one more quality block before taper.\n\nSo this week: ~37 miles, strictly MP-focused quality (no VO2 reps), long run back to 14 miles with the HR cap and no MP finish. Call it a reset. Then we go bigger again the week of Oct 19 with a long run around 18–20 and a longer MP block.\n\n## Your questions\n\n**1. Is 3:30 still sensible?**\nYes, with one caveat. The evidence from the 20-miler is strong — those last four miles rolled at 7:58–8:04/mi off tired legs, with HR climbing but not blown. That's a real signal you can hold goal pace. The caveat: we need to see one more good quality block (an 18–20 mi long run with a solid MP close, and MP intervals that stay *at* MP) before I'll say it with full confidence. We'll know in about two weeks. Keep the goal, earn the goal.\n\n**2. Heavy legs Monday after a 20-miler — normal?**\nTotally normal, especially this one. You stacked a hard interval session Thursday (max HR 184 — that's VO2 territory, not MP) and then went 20 miles with a hot finish three days later. Of course Monday felt like mud. What I'd watch: legs should feel materially better by Wednesday. If they don't, tell me and we'll cut Thursday's quality back.\n\n## About Thursday last week\nThe plan was 2 × 2 mi at goal MP (~8:00/mi). What you ran was effectively 5 × 1 mi hard with jog recovery, with the fast miles down at 6:55–7:02 — that's closer to 10K effort than MP. One red zone session won't hurt you, but we have an explicit plan to save VO2 work for later in the build (if at all). Right now, every quality mile needs to be teaching your legs *marathon pace feel*. That means staying at 7:55–8:05/mi on the fast portions, not racing them. This Thursday is the test — if you're under 7:50 on the first rep, back off.\n\n## Why each session this week\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out from the 20, flat canal, Z2 only. Walk if the first mile is grim.\n- **Wed easy 7 + 6×20s strides**: aerobic volume with a touch of leg speed. Strides are relaxed turnover, not sprints.\n- **Thu MP intervals (3 × 2 mi)**: this is the correction session. Six total MP miles at 7:55–8:05/mi with 3:00 easy jog between. More MP volume than last planned, but *at MP*, not faster. Hilly Thursday route is fine; hold effort on climbs.\n- **Sat easy 4**: pre-long flush, slower is better.\n- **Sun long 14, HR ≤150, no MP finish**: deliberate step-back. We earned a quieter Sunday. Pure aerobic time on feet so you arrive at next week fresh enough to go bigger.\n\n## Flags\n- Z1+Z2 only 68.7% last week — below the 80% base target. The culprit was Thursday's VO2 drift plus a hot long-run finish. Keep easy days genuinely easy and this fixes itself this week.\n- Long-run drift +16.9 bpm — above target. Partly heat/fatigue, partly the picked-up finish. The HR cap on Sunday is non-negotiable this week.\n- Sleep 6.8 hrs is low for someone running 45 mi/wk. Protect sleep this week — it's part of the training.\n- Shin history noted. Nothing bothering you now; if anything whispers after the 20-miler, tell me.	2026-10-11 20:01:23.142743+00	6de0ee3e-ff03-4b5c-a998-09eedbead69f	\N	15537574-8af0-49e8-9295-ce6b4e595faf	\N	\N
416a98b7-d0d9-404d-969d-a03c6f200325	athlete	Legs are heavy today after the 20 miler. Tuesday is an easy 6 as planned. Is it fine to keep it properly slow?	2026-10-13 00:10:00.043636+00	d63887bf-dede-4062-886f-8fc6cda22144	\N	\N	\N	\N
d938d66f-6fd4-4f6c-8fd0-13898550cf98	coach	Short answer: yes — properly slow is the whole point today.\n\nHeavy legs 36–48 hours after a 20 miler is normal. Your 3-week aerobic average easy HR is parked at 144 and your easy pace has been drifting faster than I'd like (9:12–9:20/mi), so this is a good day to deliberately resist that. Aim for the slow end of the window:\n\n- Pace: 9:40–9:50/mi, drift to 10:00/mi if that's what Z2 asks for. No shame in it.\n- HR: Z2, 138–148. If HR won't settle under 150 in the first mile, slow down more or walk 30s and restart.\n- Keep it on the flat canal, no strides, no pickups, no "oh my legs feel okay now let me open up." The point of today is circulation and recovery, not fitness.\n\nOne nudge on the distance — the plan has Tuesday at 5 mi, not 6. Stick to 5 (or cut to 4 if legs still feel like concrete after the first mile). We've got a 3 × 2 mi MP session Thursday that matters a lot more than an extra easy mile today, and I want you landing on it with something in the tank.\n\nQuick gut-check for the rest of the week while you're thinking about it: if Tuesday feels dead-legged start to finish, that's fine — but it tells me we drop Thursday to 2 × 2 mi instead of 3 × 2 mi. Report back after today's run and we'll call it from there. Sunday is a planned step-back to 14 easy, so there's recovery baked in.\n\nThe engine is doing what it's supposed to. Trust the slow miles.	2026-10-13 00:10:26.259506+00	d63887bf-dede-4062-886f-8fc6cda22144	\N	f4251962-d2dd-41e2-8ec4-325ae91a40ab	\N	\N
2f681b5a-e3e7-41ab-916b-b06952ded327	coach	Nice controlled run on paper. Quick numbers first, then shoes.\n\n**The run (9.15 mi, 1h 23m, 9:04/mi avg, HR 148 avg / 154 max):**\n- Splits were metronomic: 9:07, 9:07, 9:04, 9:05, 9:05, 9:06, 9:05, 9:04, 9:00. No surges, no sloppy miles.\n- HR drift: 147 → 153 across the full run. Only +6 bpm over 9 miles — that's a clean aerobic day. The engine was honest.\n- You tipped briefly into low Z3 by HR at the end (153 vs Z2 ceiling 150), but marginal and expected on a long-ish midweek.\n\nTwo things I want to flag before we get to the shoes:\n\n1. **The plan was 7 mi + 6×20s strides, not a medium-long 9.** You added two miles and dropped the strides. HR says you got away with it, but on a Wednesday two days before Sunday's long run with an MP block in it, I'd rather you give me exactly what's prescribed than freelance the volume. We build this block on consistency.\n2. **Easy pace is drifting fast again.** 9:04 is below the 9:25–9:45 window. Same nag as last week — if HR stays honest the aerobic benefit is still there, but every time "easy" creeps to 9:00 I lose a bit of the recovery stimulus. Please dial Thursday back to actual 9:30–9:50.\n\n**On the Achilles whisper at mile 7:** glad it settled, glad you told me. Given the September episode I want you watching it closely — see the note below. I'm not pulling anything out of the plan on the basis of a few minutes of noticeable sensation that cleared, but the rules in that note are firm.\n\n**On the shoe question — partial yes, with a staged introduction.**\n\nThe Endorphin Speed 4 has a different drop and more aggressive geometry than the Ghost 16. Swapping the shoe you spend the most time in — long runs — mid-block while an Achilles whisper just surfaced is the kind of change I'd normally make 8 weeks out, not 4–5. The upside you want (race-pace feel, legs that know the shoe) is real. The downside (changing calf/Achilles load pattern during a tendon-sensitive window) is also real. So:\n\n- **This Sunday (Oct 18) — Endorphin Speed 4 for the whole 15.** You get fueling-at-MP practice and shoe practice in the same run. I'm updating that day for you.\n- **The peak long run the following Sunday (17–18 mi w/ 6–8 at MP) — Endorphin Speed 4.** That's the run that most mirrors race day; you want to be in race shoes for it.\n- **Taper long runs (3 and 2 weeks out, mostly easy with a short MP touch) — Ghost 16 for the easy portion. Speed 4 for the MP touch is a reasonable swap if you want it.**\n- **Easy days (Tue/Wed/Sat) stay in Ghost 16.** The Speed 4 is a workout shoe, not a daily-easy shoe — the plate masks fatigue cues and the midsole life gets eaten quickly if you put all your miles on it.\n\n**Non-negotiables for Sunday:**\n- If the Achilles says anything louder than a whisper during the run, bail on the MP block, jog home, and we talk before Tuesday.\n- First-steps-in-the-morning check is still your canary. If morning stiffness returns and doesn't ease within a few minutes, tell me before Sunday.\n- Keep the slow heel drops going. They've been doing their job.\n\nReport back Sunday night with: (a) how the Speed 4 felt on the easy miles vs the MP block, (b) any Achilles signal during or after, (c) how fueling sat during MP effort. That answer dictates how aggressive we get on the peak long run.	2026-10-14 22:45:37.285587+00	d63887bf-dede-4062-886f-8fc6cda22144	\N	7e3372a9-247a-4614-b2c5-8ec53f78f556	3	[{"body": "A brief whisper at mile 7 that settled is OK information, not an alarm — but the September history earns it a closer watch. Pull the plug on a run and talk to your sports physio before the next session if any of these show up: morning first-steps stiffness that doesn't ease within a few minutes, sharpening pain while running, pain that alters your stride, swelling or tenderness when you squeeze the tendon, or symptoms that worsen run-over-run. I coach the training; a clinician diagnoses the tissue. Don't guess on this one.", "title": "Achilles — monitor, don't push through", "severity": "caution"}]
